NotesString/You can open this notes window for any project by selecting View->Notes...

This example shows data and control signals captured from a 125MHz SDRAM 
controller used in a DSP system. The LogicPort sampled the data at 500MHz 
yielding 2ns resolution. 

There are several points of interest in this data which have each been marked 
by individual cursors. You can quickly scroll to a cursor location by typing its 
letter on the keyboard. You can also return to the trigger location by typing T 
on the keyboard.

This particular SDRAM controller requires two phases of a 125MHz clock offset 
by 90 degrees (2ns). If you zoom in several levels by pressing the + key on 
your keypad, you can see that the LogicPort has no trouble displaying this 2ns 
phase shift. You can zoom back out using the - key on the keypad, or by using 
a button in the toolbar. See the help file for several other zoom/scroll methods.

Acquisition was triggered on the 1000th refresh pulse (REFRESH_ACK) from 
the SDRAM controller. Because the system under test is of a very dynamic 
nature, we triggered on the 1000th refresh pulse to give it time to stabilize 
before capturing the data. The control signals for the SDRAM have been 
combined into a group called "SDRAM Commands". Using this group we can 
easily decode the standard commands which are being issued to the SDRAM 
by the controller. If you look at the data at cursor E, you can see that the value 
is 1h which equates to a refresh command.  

The goal of this troubleshooting session was to find out why the output FIFO for 
the DSP system was underflowing. You can see from the low going pulses on 
the OUTPUT_VALID signal that it is underflowing on a regular basis. The 
suspicion was that it was underflowing after SDRAM refresh cycles. You can 
see from the data that it is occurring at times when no refresh was issued. The 
problem turned out to be on the FIFO_WR signal generated by logic external to 
the controller. At cursor C you can see that the FIFO_WR signal is high for a 
shorter period than the RD_DATA_VALID signal from which it is derived. Each 
of these short pulses caused some data to be missed, starving the FIFO. Each 
of the short pulses is followed by an underflow indication as shown at cursor D 
(note that there is a considerable pipeline depth in the associated logic).

We have also setup the LogicPort's cursor measurements at the bottom of the 
display window to get some statistics from the controller. Measurement A is 
set to measure the average duty cycle for D5, which is the WR_DATA_REQ 
signal. A duty cycle of 0.265 indicates that the controller is writing data to the 
SDRAM 26.5% of the time. Measurement B is set to measure D4, which is the 
RD_DATA_VALID signal. The average duty cycle value of 0.157381 indicates 
that the controller is reading data from the SDRAM only 15.7% of the time 
when writing to the FIFO. The inequality of these values is a reflection of the 
fact that the data being written and read from the SDRAM are not directly 
related in the system under test.

Please note that all of the example files are "read only" so that they remain 
intact for future reference. You can change settings in these files for learning 
purposes, but the changes cannot be saved. You can however use the 
"Save As" option to save an editable copy of any example file.
/
